Pace Time: 28.4" [:br {}] "Width: 19.0 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The defending Olympic and reigning World Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/127739"} "Perrine Laffont"] " of France, was present in the women’s moguls event at the 2022 Beijing Olympics and considered a favorite. Also appearing was the 2014 Olympic champion and 2018 runner-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/127456"} "Justine Dufour-Lapointe"] " of Canada, and Kazakhstan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/119042"} "Yuliya Galysheva"] ", the 2018 bronze medalist, 2019 World Champion, and 2021 World runner-up. The 2021 World bronze medalist was " [:a {:href "/athletes/148799"} "Anastasiya Smirnova"] " of Russia. Also in contention, based on their performance at the World Cup, were Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/148527"} "Anri Kawamura"] " and Australia’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/136680"} "Jakara Anthony"] ". Anthony, the 2019 World runner-up, had just missed the podium in fourth at the 2018 PyeongChang Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "All of the favorites qualified for the first round of the final, led by Anthony, Laffont, and American " [:a {:href "/athletes/138286"} "Jaelin Kauf"] ". Kauf had competed in this event in 2018 and placed seventh. In the first final, Dufour-Lapointe crashed, ending her medal chances, while the rest advanced to the third round of the final, except Galyasheva, who was out in round two. Anthony, who had been leading the entire way, did not let up in the final and captured the gold medal. Perhaps more surprising was Kauf and Smirnova taking the next two places, leaving Laffont and Kawamura off the podium entirely."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying Round 1"] [:th {} "Qualifying Round 2"] [:th {} "Final Round 1"] [:th {} "Final Round 2"] [:th {} "Final Round 3"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3"]